  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nWe have at least one more day of pleasant Fall weather and dry \nconditions as the 500mb blocking pattern, the pair of high pressure \nsystems over the Great Lakes and the Gulf coast, keeps the ridge \nover our area from moving out quickly. However, upper level \nsaturation will bring clouds back into our area throughout the day \nThursday. This cloud cover and light easterly flow at the surface \nwill keep temperatures in the 70s today.\n\nOvernight, the ridge over the central US begins to weaken as a \nshortwave trough starts to develop over the Rockies. An associated \nsurface low begins to develop and the high pressure to our NE begins \nto exit, supporting southerly flow to our west bringing warm moist \nair. Over our area, isentropic ascent will trigger the initial lift \nneeded for rain in our area, primarily in NW MO. There are no major \nconcerns for thunderstorms Friday morning because the low-level \nclouds and weak lapse rates will keep instability low. 500mb heights \ncontinue to fall Friday night into Saturday as the shortwave and \ndeepening surface low progress east which will support better lift \nand better chances (70-90% north of I-70 and 50-70% south) for rain \nacross the entire CWA with a 20-30% chance for isolated \nthunderstorms primarily along the KS/MO border north of I-70. Rain \nwill stick around for much of the weekend as another blocking \npattern forms at 500mb as high pressure over the Great Lakes is \nsandwiched between a developing Nor'easter over the Mid-Atlantic and \na deepening trough over the Pacific NW. Southerly flow will continue \nto bring warmer air into our region and the 500mb ridge starts to \nbuild back into the area Sunday so rain chances will diminish \nthroughout the day Sunday. However, this ridge will have to contend \nwith the Nor'easter that sets up shop off the North Atlantic coast \nand tries to stick around early next week. This could keep the ridge \nfrom progressing as far east, keeping rain chances around for a \nlittle longer and keeping us cooler. If the ridge is able to \nprogress into our area Monday we could see slightly warmer \ntemperatures in the mid 80s. \n\nWe are also watching for another chance of rain in the middle of \nnext week as models have the 500mb trough over the Pacific NW \ndeepening and moving into the central US.The exact timing of this \nsystem however, will depend on how quickly the Nor'easter moves off \nof the NE Atlantic coast.",
